// ForwardGuidex — Firestore security rules.
//
// Firestore here is a server-only, create-only history archive
// (`snapshots_history/{date}_{artifact_sha256}`). There is NO browser Firebase
// SDK and NO public Firestore access anywhere in this project (see the
// architecture doc: "static-first... no Firebase SDK / public DB rules in the
// browser"). All reads/writes happen server-side in GitHub Actions via the
// Admin SDK (`firebase-admin`), authenticated through Workload Identity
// Federation (WIF) — never via a client SDK, never with a service-account key
// file, and never from this database's own client-auth rules.
//
// Distinct WIF service accounts enforce least privilege at the IAM layer
// (writer SA: Firestore create-only via `serve/publish.py`; reader SA:
// read-only, used by the CI last-known-good retrieval step). These Firestore
// rules are the second, redundant layer: deny every client request outright,
// regardless of caller, because no legitimate caller is ever a browser/client
// SDK. If that ever changes (e.g. a future browser-live path), these rules
// must be redesigned with narrow, audited scopes — not relaxed to `if true`.
rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
